    "captions": [
      { "t": 0.0, "text": "Summer 52 BC. Caesar traps Vercingetorix and, by Caesar's count, 80,000 Gauls in the hilltop town of Alesia, between two rivers." },
      { "t": 0.6, "text": "Instead of storming it, the Romans dig. An inner ring of ditches, ramparts and towers, 11 Roman miles round, seals the town in." },
      { "t": 1.2, "text": "Knowing a huge relief army is coming, Caesar builds a second ring, 14 miles round, facing outward. His army is now besieger and besieged." },
      { "t": 1.7, "text": "Food runs short. The Gauls send the town's women and children out; Caesar will not let them through, and they are left between the walls." },
      { "t": 2.1, "text": "The relief army arrives from the west: 250,000 infantry and 8,000 cavalry, Caesar claims, almost certainly an exaggeration." },
      { "t": 2.5, "text": "A great cavalry battle on the plain. Caesar's German horsemen break the Gallic cavalry." },
      { "t": 3.1, "text": "Attacks on the outer wall are thrown back. At night Vercassivellaunus leads 60,000 picked men round to the north, toward the camp below Mont Réa, where the walls are weakest." },
      { "t": 4.4, "text": "At noon they attack the camp from outside while Vercingetorix attacks from inside. Caesar sends Labienus with six cohorts to hold it." },
      { "t": 5.0, "text": "Caesar, recognisable by his scarlet cloak, leads cavalry out round the walls and falls on the attackers from behind. The relief army collapses." },
      { "t": 5.7, "text": "Next day Vercingetorix surrenders. Gaul is Rome's. Six years later he is paraded in Caesar's triumph and executed." }
    ]
